The sphere of unit radius centered at the origin is given by \[x^2+y^2+z^2=1.\] For a sphere centered at the origin, the outward normal vector at a point \((x,y,z)\) is along the radius vector. The position vector of the point is \[\vec{r}=x\hat{i}+y\hat{j}+z\hat{k}.\] Its magnitude on the unit sphere is \[|\vec{r}|=\sqrt{x^2+y^2+z^2}=1.\] Hence, the unit normal vector is \[\hat{n}
=
\frac{\vec{r}}{|\vec{r}|}
=
(x,y,z).\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) (x,y,z)}}\]

Given, \[x(t)=3+2\sin t \cos 2t\] Using \[2\sin A \cos B=\sin(A+B)+\sin(A-B)\] \[x(t)=3+\sin 3t-\sin t\] Therefore, \[x_{\text{rms}}
=\sqrt{3^2+\frac{1^2}{2}+\frac{(-1)^2}{2}}
=\sqrt{10}\] \[\boxed{\text{C) $\sqrt{10}$}}\]

Maximum power is transferred from circuit \(A\) to circuit \(B\), if circuit \(B\) offers a resistance of \(2~\Omega\) to circuit \(A\). <img src="img/a250824f01e46009.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,320px)"> Thevenin's equivalent of circuit \(A\) is given by, <img src="img/77e3743f686b3aab.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,224px)"> From Fig.~(b), \[I=\frac{10}{2+2}=2.5~\text{A}\] and \[V_{PQ}=I\times2
=2.5\times2
=5~\text{V}\] From Fig.~(a), \[I_{R_L}=\frac{V_{PQ}-3}{R_L}\] \[I_{R_L}=\frac{5-3}{R_L}
=\frac{2}{R_L}\] Also, \[I_{R_L}=I\] \[\frac{2}{R_L}=2.5\] \[R_L=\frac{2}{2.5}
=0.8~\Omega\] (C) \(0.8~\Omega\)

For given ideal op-amp, the non-inverting terminal is grounded. Hence, the inverting terminal is also at virtual ground. \[V_{-}=0\] <img src="img/b62e394543e1af08.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Current through resistor \(R\): \[I_{R}=\frac{V_{i}-0}{R}=\frac{V_{i}}{R}\] Applying KCL at the inverting node, \[\frac{V_{i}}{R}=\frac{0-V_{o}}{3R}\] \[V_{o}=-3V_{i}\] The input current is the sum of currents through \(R\) and \(2R\): \[I_{i}=\frac{V_{i}}{R}+\frac{V_{i}-V_{o}}{2R}\] Substituting \(V_{o}=-3V_{i}\), \[I_{i}=\frac{V_{i}}{R}+\frac{V_{i}+3V_{i}}{2R}
=\frac{V_{i}}{R}+\frac{2V_{i}}{R}
=\frac{3V_{i}}{R}\] Therefore, \[R_{in}=\frac{V_{i}}{I_{i}}
=\frac{V_{i}}{3V_{i}/R}
=\frac{R}{3}\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) } \dfrac{R}{3}}\]

For an ideal op-amp, the non-inverting terminal is grounded. Hence, the inverting terminal is at virtual ground. Initially, neglect the Zener branch: <img src="img/aa2af06c679b60f6.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> \[V_{o}=-\frac{10\,\text{k}\Omega}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}V_{i}
=-10\times 1=-10\,\text{V}\] Since this output is large enough, the Zener diode goes into breakdown. The Zener branch now provides an additional feedback path. <img src="img/e088675941e49c76.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> Since the Zener conducts, the output must satisfy \[-10\,\text{V}&lt;V_{o}&lt;-3.2\,\text{V}\] Assume \(V_{o}=-5\,\text{V}\): \[i_{1}=\frac{5}{10}=0.5\,\text{mA},\qquad
i_{2}=\frac{5-3.2}{10}=0.18\,\text{mA}\] \[i_{1}+i_{2}=0.68\,\text{mA}\neq1\,\text{mA}\] Hence, this value is not possible. Now assume \(V_{o}=-6.6\,\text{V}\): \[i_{1}=\frac{6.6}{10}=0.66\,\text{mA}\] \[i_{2}=\frac{6.6-3.2}{10}=0.34\,\text{mA}\] \[i_{1}+i_{2}=0.66+0.34=1\,\text{mA}\] This satisfies KCL at the inverting node. \[\boxed{\text{(b) }-6.6\,\text{V}}\]

The capacitor is initially charged to \[V_{c}=15\,\text{V}\] since the switch has been at Position 1 for a long time. The zener diodes clamp the output voltage to \[V_{o}=+5\,\text{V}.\] The non-inverting terminal is connected to the midpoint of two equal resistors. Hence, \[V_{+}=\frac{5}{2}=2.5\,\text{V}.\] The output changes state when the capacitor voltage falls to \[V_{c}=2.5\,\text{V}.\] The capacitor is discharged by a constant current through the \(1\,\text{k}\Omega\) resistor, \[I=\frac{V_{+}}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
=\frac{2.5}{1\,\text{k}\Omega}
=2.5\,\text{mA}.\] Using \[I=C\frac{dV_{c}}{dt},\] \[10^{-6}\left(\frac{15-2.5}{dt}\right)=2.5\times10^{-3}.\] Therefore, \[dt=\frac{10^{-6}\times12.5}{2.5\times10^{-3}}
=5\times10^{-3}\,\text{s}
=5\,\text{ms}.\] \[\boxed{\text{(a) }5.00\,\text{ms}}\]

Since the collector current of \(Q_3\) is \[I_{C3}=2\,\text{mA},\] the tail current is equally shared by the matched differential pair: \[I_{C1}=I_{C2}=1\,\text{mA}\] Hence, \[g_m=\frac{I_C}{V_T}
=\frac{1\,\text{mA}}{25\,\text{mV}}
=0.04\,\text{S}\] The collector resistor is \[R_C=R_2=1\,\text{k}\Omega\] For a differential pair with one input grounded and an ideal tail current source, the single-ended voltage gain is \[A_v
=
-\frac{g_mR_C}{2}\] Substituting, \[A_v
=
-\frac{0.04\times1000}{2}
=
-20\] Therefore, \[\boxed{\text{(a) }-20}\]

The integer part of \(1.375\) is \(1\), which is \((1)_2\) directly. Converting the fractional part \((0.375)_{10}\) to binary by successive multiplication by 2: \[\begin{array}{lll}
            0.375 \times 2 = 0.750 &amp; \rightarrow &amp; 0 \\
            0.75  \times 2 = 1.50  &amp; \rightarrow &amp; 1 \\
            0.50  \times 2 = 1.00  &amp; \rightarrow &amp; 1 \\
        \end{array}
        \quad \implies \quad (0.375)_{10} = (0.011)_2\] Combining both parts: \[(1)_{10} + (0.375)_{10} = (1)_2 + (0.011)_2 = (1.011)_2\] \[\boxed{(C)\; 1.011}\] <div class="sol-block sol-mistakes"><div class="sol-block-title">Mistakes to avoid</div><ul><li>Do not stop the multiplication early; continue until the fractional part becomes exactly \(0.00\) or a repeating pattern is detected.</li></ul></div>

The plant transfer function is, \[G_p(s)=\frac{2}{s(s+3)}\] The PI controller with \(K_p=1\) is, \[G_c(s)=K_p+\frac{K_i}{s}
=1+\frac{K_i}{s}
=\frac{s+K_i}{s}\] Therefore, the open-loop transfer function is, \[G(s)=G_c(s)G_p(s)
=\left(\frac{s+K_i}{s}\right)\left(\frac{2}{s(s+3)}\right)
=\frac{2(s+K_i)}{s^2(s+3)}\] For a unit step input, \(e_{ss}=\dfrac{1}{1+K},\) where the position error constant is \(K=\lim\limits_{s\to0}G(s).\) If \(K_i=0\), \[G(s)=\frac{2}{s(s+3)},\] which is a Type-1 system. \[K=\lim_{s\to0}\frac{2}{s(s+3)}
=\infty\] and therefore \(e_{ss}=\dfrac{1}{1+\infty}=0\) If \(K_i&gt;0\), \[K=\lim_{s\to0}\frac{2(s+K_i)}{s^2(s+3)}
=\infty\] and hence \(e_{ss}=\dfrac{1}{1+\infty}=0\) Thus, the steady-state error is zero for both \(K_i&gt;0\) and \(K_i=0\). We now verify the stability at the boundary value \(K_i=0\). The characteristic equation becomes, \[1+\frac{2}{s(s+3)}=0\] \[s(s+3)+2=0\] \[s^2+3s+2=0\] \[(s+1)(s+2)=0\] The closed-loop poles are \(s=-1,\,-2,\) which lie entirely in the left-half of the \(s\)-plane. Hence, the closed-loop system is stable even when \(K_i=0\). \[\boxed{(A)\;0}\]

To determine the feedforward controller, consider only the disturbance input. Hence, \(r(t)=0.\) Let \(P(s)=\dfrac{1}{s(s+1)}\) The output due to the disturbance is, \[Y(s)=\frac{P(s)G_{ff}(s)+1}{1+P(s)}\,D(s)\] To eliminate the effect of the disturbance at \(\omega=1\,\text{rad/s},\) the numerator must be zero. \[P(j1)G_{ff}(j1)+1=0\] \[G_{ff}(j1)=-\frac{1}{P(j1)}\] Now, \[P(j1)=\frac{1}{j(1+j)}
=\frac{1}{-1+j}
=\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\angle-\frac{3\pi}{4}\] Therefore, \[G_{ff}(j1)
=-\sqrt2~\angle\frac{3\pi}{4}
=\sqrt2~\angle-\frac{\pi}{4}\] \[\boxed{(D)\;\sqrt2\angle-\dfrac{\pi}{4}}\]

Let \(P(s)=\dfrac{1}{s(s+1)}\) Let the PD controller be, \[G_{ff}(s)=K_P+sK_D\] \[G_{ff}(j\omega)=K_P+j\omega K_D\] From Q10.09.2, \[G_{ff}(j1)=\sqrt2~\angle-\frac{\pi}{4}\] Hence, \[K_P=\sqrt2\times \cos\left(-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=1 \qquad \text{and} \qquad \omega K_D=K_D=\sqrt2\times \sin\left(-\frac{\pi}{4}\right)=-1\] Therefore, \[G_{ff}(j\omega)=K_P+j\omega K_D=1-j\omega\] The output due to the disturbance is, \[Y(s)=\frac{P(s)G_{ff}(s)+1}{1+P(s)}\,D(s)\] \[\frac{Y(s)}{D(s)}=\frac{P(s)G_{ff}(s)+1}{1+P(s)}
=\frac{s^2+(1+K_D)s+K_P}{s^2+s+1}
=\frac{s^2+1}{s^2+s+1}\] \[\frac{Y(j\omega)}{D(j\omega)}=\frac{1-\omega^2}{1-\omega^2+j\omega}\] For the disturbance input \(d(t)=sin(2t)\), the output amplitude will be: \[|y(t)| =1 \times  \left| \frac{1-(2)^2}{1-(2)^2+j(2)}\right|= \left| \frac{-3}{-3+j2}\right|=\frac{3}{\sqrt{13}}=\sqrt{\frac{9}{13}}\] \[\boxed{(B)\;\sqrt{\dfrac{9}{13}}}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li>If the input to a transfer function \(G(s)\) is \(x(t)=A\sin(\omega_0 t+\phi),\) then the steady-state output is: \(y(t)=A|G(j\omega_0)|\sin\!\left(\omega_0 t+\phi+\angle G(j\omega_0)\right)\)</li><li>Here, the amplitude of the output is: \(A|G(j\omega_0)|\) and phase of the output is: \(\phi+\angle G(j\omega_0)\)</li></ul></div>
